        <title>Every Drill Passed. The First Real Order Broke Everything: PopHonest&#39;s Payment Chain, As It Happened</title>
        <link>https://gowinder.work/en/pophonest-payment-chain/</link>
        <pubDate>Sat, 28 Nov 2026 09:00:00 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://gowinder.work/en/pophonest-payment-chain/</guid>
        <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;first-what-this-is&#34;&gt;First, what this is
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://pophonest.com&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;PopHonest&lt;/a&gt; is a WordPress popup plugin I made. Popups have a bad name — fake countdowns, fake stock levels, &amp;ldquo;only 2 left!&amp;rdquo; — so the plugin&amp;rsquo;s whole pitch is the word in its name: Honest. Popups when they should pop, no dark patterns. Core is $149 a year, Pro is $249 a year, payments run through Waffo, and activation is a licence key.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The product itself isn&amp;rsquo;t much to talk about. This post is about its payment chain, because the thing that taught me a lesson wasn&amp;rsquo;t building the plugin. It was taking money.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        <item>
        <title>I Built a Cost Simulator for My Agent Runs, Then Killed the Paid Version</title>
        <link>https://gowinder.work/en/gemini-cost-simulator-free/</link>
        <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 09:00:00 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://gowinder.work/en/gemini-cost-simulator-free/</guid>
        <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;you-dont-feel-it-while-the-money-burns&#34;&gt;You don&amp;rsquo;t feel it while the money burns
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;I run agents every day now. Read this code, change that file, run the tests, round after round. It flows so well that money never crosses my mind.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The problem is that an agent&amp;rsquo;s bill doesn&amp;rsquo;t work like &amp;ldquo;one question, one price.&amp;rdquo; Every extra round makes the context longer, and everything before it gets fed back in. Then there&amp;rsquo;s thinking, which is billed at the output rate — the deeper it thinks, the more it costs. At the end of the month I get one total. Which run burned the most, and why? No idea.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        <item>
        <title>Muse Is in the Cloud, My Code Is on My Mac</title>
        <link>https://gowinder.work/en/muse-agent-bridge-mcp/</link>
        <pubDate>Tue, 24 Nov 2026 09:00:00 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://gowinder.work/en/muse-agent-bridge-mcp/</guid>
        <description>&lt;figure class=&#34;loveit-image-shim&#34;&gt;
  &lt;img src=&#34;https://gowinder.work/images/muse-agent-bridge-workflow-en.svg&#34; alt=&#34;The Muse × Agent Bridge workflow: I set the spec, Muse writes specs, dispatches and verifies from the cloud, the work travels over MCP and an encrypted relay into a hosted session on my Mac, and the result comes back to me&#34; width=&#34;640&#34; title=&#34;I set the spec, Muse dispatches and verifies, the work happens on the Mac, and the result comes back to me — MCP and an always-on listener close the loop.&#34;&gt;
  &lt;figcaption&gt;I set the spec, Muse dispatches and verifies, the work happens on the Mac, and the result comes back to me — MCP and an always-on listener close the loop.&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quick note on where this post sits, so it doesn&amp;rsquo;t blur into the previous two. In the November 5 post, I was the one driving the agents on my machine. The November 15 post was the &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://agent-bridge.craftstead.app&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Agent Bridge&lt;/a&gt; release post, about the tool itself. This one is about the MCP piece that came later: the driver is Muse now, and I&amp;rsquo;m mostly left with deciding things and looking at results.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        <item>
        <title>Away From the Desk: I Gave My Mac Agents a Phone Entrance, and Why &#34;Yes, Merge It&#34; on a Phone Doesn&#39;t Count</title>
        <link>https://gowinder.work/en/agent-bridge/</link>
        <pubDate>Sun, 15 Nov 2026 20:00:00 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://gowinder.work/en/agent-bridge/</guid>
        <description>&lt;p&gt;The last post was about the result file, how the coordinator and the executor hand work over. That setup works, but I skipped past a precondition at the time: the executor runs on my machine, so the human has to be at my machine too. Later I moved the heavy work back onto my own laptop, and that precondition turned into a daily problem — the work lives on the Mac, and I am often not in front of the Mac.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        <item>
        <title>Grok Bot, Claude Code, and DeepSeek Harness: one result file that saves most of the tokens</title>
        <link>https://gowinder.work/en/agent-handoff-result-file/</link>
        <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://gowinder.work/en/agent-handoff-result-file/</guid>
        <description>&lt;p&gt;I keep a handful of Grok bots across a few projects: a game, some small SEO sites, a few MCP tools of my own. They plan and schedule. The actual work, writing code, running tests, clicking through a browser, goes to two agents on my Mac: dsh in a sandbox, and Claude Code via &lt;code&gt;claude -p&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Heavy work on the laptop, coordination in the cloud. The seam between them was the problem. Bots bill by the token, and a long context is both expensive and dumber. The first version of the handoff was me copy-pasting: I would run dsh or Claude Code myself and paste the terminal output back into the chat. The delay was tolerable. The cost was not. That block of output sat in every later turn, and the bot would read logs and think and slowly lose track of what it had said ten minutes earlier.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        <item>
        <title>Batch renaming files from an Excel or CSV list: three approaches that actually work</title>
        <link>https://gowinder.work/en/batch-renaming-files-from-excel-csv/</link>
        <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://gowinder.work/en/batch-renaming-files-from-excel-csv/</guid>
        <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;batch-renaming-files-from-an-excel-or-csv-list&#34;&gt;Batch renaming files from an Excel or CSV list
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;If you are cleaning up photos, scans, lecture videos or invoices, the situation is usually the same: &lt;strong&gt;you already have a table&lt;/strong&gt;. Column A is the current filename, column B is what you want it to be, maybe column C is a capture date and D is a client code. The hard part is not inventing new names. It is turning that table into actual renames on disk.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        <item>
        <title>Two small sites went live: an Old English dictionary and a batch renamer</title>
        <link>https://gowinder.work/en/two-small-sites/</link>
        <pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 14:30:00 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://gowinder.work/en/two-small-sites/</guid>
        <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;two-small-sites-went-live-an-old-english-dictionary-and-a-batch-renamer&#34;&gt;Two small sites went live: an Old English dictionary and a batch renamer
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;I wanted to test one question: can one person, with no budget and no ads, get anywhere by taking a very narrow need all the way to the end?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rather than argue about it, I built two things. They sit at opposite ends of the spectrum — one is pure content, one is a pure tool — which makes them a clean control group.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        <item>
        <title>Projects</title>
        <link>https://gowinder.work/en/projects/</link>
        <pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0800</pubDate>
        
        <guid>https://gowinder.work/en/projects/</guid>
        <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;projects&#34;&gt;Projects
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Small, single-purpose things I build and run myself.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;eldertonguecomhttpseldertonguecom&#34;&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://eldertongue.com&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;eldertongue.com&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;An Old English dictionary. Form, part of speech, gloss, example, cognates — organised for people actually reading Old English texts rather than browsing an archive. Close to twenty thousand entries, statically generated and served from the edge.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;massrenamecomhttpsmassrenamecom&#34;&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://massrename.com&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;massrename.com&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;A batch file renamer that works in the browser. Stack rename rules, preview every result before anything is touched, then run it once. Built for the cases where &amp;ldquo;add a prefix&amp;rdquo; is not enough and a shell script is too much. One-time purchase.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        <item>
